# In the Hall of the Mountain King (repertoire plan: hall-of-the-mountain-king). The main theme of
# Grieg's piece from Peer Gynt, as it is commonly printed, moved from B minor down a fifth to
# E minor. Only the four-bar theme is used (E F# G A B G B | A# F# A# A F A | E F# G A B G B E |
# D B G B D), with the composer's chromatic notes A# and F: the thirds A#-F# then A-F creep down
# by half steps. As in the original, the theme comes round again and again, each time faster: the
# tempo steps up from 60 to 160, one step per statement. The first two statements are low, in
# first position (as the bassoons and cellos play it); then the theme moves up an octave into
# second position (finger 1 on fret 2), where every note is fretted, so each one can be cut short
# by easing off the finger. Every eighth and quarter is staccato; only the D that ends each
# statement is held, for two beats. The final E minor chord is this arrangement's own ending (the
# orchestra ends on loud chords).
# The notes are the theme as it is commonly printed (Grieg's B minor: B C# D E F# D F# | E# C# E#
# E C E | B C# D E F# D F# B | A F# D F# A). They have not yet been checked against a scan of an
# early edition, which the repertoire plan asks for.
title: In the Hall of the Mountain King
composer: Edvard Grieg
source: Peer Gynt, incidental music (1875), "In the Hall of the Mountain King", main theme; arranged in E minor for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (Grieg died in 1907); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 5
tags: grieg, classical, staccato, accelerando, chromatic, halloween
tempo: 60
time: 4/4
key: Em

